A Cache Stampede (also called Dogpile Effect) happens when many requests miss the same cache entry simultaneously, causing all of them to hit the DB.

Example:

Redis:
Product:123 expires

1000 users request it at the same time.

Without protection:

1000 Requests
      |
Cache Miss
      |
1000 DB Queries

The DB gets overwhelmed.

Common Causes
Cache TTL expires.
Cache server restarts.
Hot key evicted due to memory pressure.
Solutions
1. Distributed Lock (Most Common)

First request:

Cache Miss
     |
Acquire Redis Lock ✅
     |
Read DB
     |
Update Cache
     |
Release Lock

Other requests:

Cache Miss
     |
Lock exists
     |
Wait / Retry after 50ms

Only one DB query happens.


Value getProduct(id) {

    // 1. Try cache
    val = redis.get(id);
    if (val != NULL)
        return val;

    // 2. Try distributed lock
    if (redis.tryLock("lock:" + id)) {

        try {
            // Someone else may have populated while we acquired lock
            val = redis.get(id);
            if (val != NULL)
                return val;

            // Read DB
            val = db.get(id);

            // Populate cache
            redis.set(id, val, TTL);

            return val;
        }
        finally {
            redis.unlock("lock:" + id);
        }
    }

    // 3. Another server is already fetching from DB
    while (true) {

        sleep(50ms);

        val = redis.get(id);

        if (val != NULL)
            return val;
    }
}



2. TTL Jitter

Instead of:

All keys expire at 10:00

use:

10:00 ± random(0-60 sec)

So millions of keys don't expire together.


3. Never Expire Hot Keys

For very popular items:

Movie Catalog
Product List

Refresh them in the background instead of letting them expire.

